\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSNotification objects encapsulate information so that it can be broadcast to other objects by an NSNotificationCenter object.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Notifications and their Rationale\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The standard way to pass information between objects is message passing\'d0one object invokes the method of another object. However, message passing requires that the object sending the message know who the receiver is and what messages it responds to. At times, this tight coupling of two objects is undesirable\'d0most notably because it would join together two otherwise independent subsystems. For these cases, a broadcast model is introduced: An object posts a notification, which is dispatched to the appropriate observers through an NSNotificationCenter object, or simply \i notification center\i0 . \
\fs14\
\fs28 An NSNotification object (referred to as a\i  notification\i0 ) contains a name, an object, and an optional dictionary. The name is a tag identifying the notification. The object is any object that the poster of the notification wants to send to observers of that notification (typically, it is the object that posted the notification). The dictionary stores other related objects if any. \
\fs14\
\fs28 Any object may post a notification. Other objects can register themselves as observers to receive notifications when they are posted. The object posting the notification, the object included in the notification, and the observer of the notification may all be different objects or the same object. Objects that post notifications need not know anything about the observers. On the other hand, observers need to know at least the notification name and keys to the dictionary if provided.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSNotification objects are immutable objects.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Notification Centers\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The notification center manages the sending and receiving of notifications. When an object wants to receive a certain notification, it registers itself with the notification center. When an object has a notification to send, it sends it to the notification center. When the notification center receives a notification, it passes that notification along to all objects registered to receive it. (See the NSNotificationCenter class specification for more on posting notifications.)\
\fs14\
\fs28 This notification model frees an object from concern about what objects it should send information to. Any object may simply post a notification without knowing what objects\'d0if any\'d0are receiving the notification. However, objects receiving notifications do need to know at least the notification name if not the type of information the notification contains. The notification center takes care of broadcasting notifications to registered observers. Another benefit of this model is to allow multiple objects to listen for notifications, which would otherwise be cumbersome.\
\fs14\
\fs28 You can create a notification object with the class methods \b notificationWithName:object:\b0  or \b notificationWithName:object:userInfo:\b0 . However, you don\'27t usually create your own notifications directly. The NSNotificationCenter methods \b postNotificationName:object:\b0  and \b postNotificationName:object:userInfo: \b0 allow you to conveniently post a notification without creating it first. \
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Notification and Delegation\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Using the notification system is similar to using delegates, but it has these advantages:\
\fs14\
\pard \s5 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx6300 \tx6590 \tx15154 \fs28 \'b7\tab Any number of objects may receive the notification, not just the delegate object. This precludes returning a value.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab An object may receive any message you like from the notification center, not just the predefined delegate methods. \
\fs14\
\fs28 \'b7\tab The object posting the notification does not even have to know the observer exists.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql NSCopying Protocol\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The NSNotification class adopts the NSCopying protocol, making it possible to treat notifications as context-independent values that can be copied and reused. You can store a notification for later use or use the Distributed Objects system to send a notification to another process. The NSCopying protocol essentially allows clients to deal with notifications as first class values that can be copied by collections. You can put notifications in an array and send the \b copy\b0  message to that array, which recursively copies every item.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Creating Subclasses\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 You can subclass NSNotification to contain information in addition to the notification name, object, and dictionary. This extra data must be agreed upon between notifiers and observers.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSNotification is actually a class cluster. As such, it provides no storage for the name, object, and userInfo values. To subclass NSNotification, you must override the primitive methods \b name\b0 , \b object\b0 , and \b userInfo\b0 . Use the \b init\b0  method as the designated initializer. \
\fs14\
